# Names and empty values only — never a real secret. See docs/12-deployment.md §2.

# Two spellings of one database, not two databases. Locally both name the Neon
# `develop` branch; in production, the Neon `main` branch. They differ only by
# `-pooler` on the leftmost label of the host, and both carry
# sslmode=verify-full and channel_binding=require — whatever the Neon dashboard
# hands out, which is sslmode=require. See docs/12-deployment.md §2.1 and §2.2.

# The POOLED (`-pooler`) host. Read by the app — every route, page and query
# helper — and by the integration and browser suites with it, so the pooler is
# exercised every day rather than only in the environment that cannot be
# debugged from.
DATABASE_URL=

# The DIRECT host. Read by `drizzle-kit migrate` and `npm run seed`, and by
# nothing else: migration tools take session-level advisory locks and issue
# SET/RESET, neither supported on a pooled connection, and the seed holds one
# long multi-statement transaction. There is no fallback to DATABASE_URL — a
# runner missing this fails loudly rather than migrating over the pooler.
DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED=

# Not used yet — sign-in is a later slice. Listed so the file stays the one
# place that says what the app will need.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=
BETTER_AUTH_URL=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
ALLOWED_EMAILS=
SENTRY_DSN=
